    {
      "type": "learn",
      "title": "GroupJoin: keep the lonely ones",
      "body": [
        "`Join` cannot tell you which courses nobody took, because unmatched items vanish. `GroupJoin` keeps **every** item on the left and hands you the (possibly empty) group of matches from the right:",
        "",
        "```csharp fragment",
        "courses.GroupJoin(scores,",
        "    c => c.Name,",
        "    s => s.Course,",
        "    (c, matches) => new { c.Name, Count = matches.Count() })",
        "```",
        "",
        "A course with `Count == 0` has no scores. This is the shape of a SQL **left join**: all of the left side, with whatever matches on the right."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "mcq",
      "prompt": "Which query lists every course along with how many scores it has, including courses with zero?",
      "options": [
        "`courses.GroupJoin(scores, c => c.Name, s => s.Course, (c, m) => (c.Name, m.Count()))`",
        "`courses.Join(scores, c => c.Name, s => s.Course, (c, s) => (c.Name, 1))`",
        "`scores.GroupBy(s => s.Course).Select(g => (g.Key, g.Count()))`",
        "`scores.Join(courses, s => s.Course, c => c.Name, (s, c) => c.Name).Distinct()`"
      ],
      "answer": 0,
      "explain": "Only `GroupJoin` starts from every course and keeps it even when nothing matches. `GroupBy` over scores can only see courses that have scores, and `Join` drops the unmatched ones."
    },
